      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "extension-via-dorian-13th",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm teaches that Dorian (rather than natural minor) is the correct scale for deriving full min7 extensions because natural minor produces a wrong 13th -- the scalar 6th of Dorian becomes the chordal 13th, extending Dm7 to Dm13. This mapping of scale degrees to extension numbers is shown with a diagram in Chapter 6, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "chord-within-chord-improv-on-min7",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes soloing over Dm7 by playing purely within Am9 arpeggio tones (A, C, E, G, B), because from the 5th of Dm13 upward the tones match Am9 exactly -- accessing the upper extensions of the original chord. This chord-within-chord approach is the signature improv move taught in Chapter 6, p. 55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p.55"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "sus4-substitution-improv",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes substituting Dm7 ideas over G7sus4 because the sus4 chord is structurally equivalent to a minor seventh built on its 5th -- playing Dm7 or Dm9 over G7sus4 naturally accesses the suspended dominant's upper extensions (G9sus4, G13sus4). Taught in Chapter 6, p. 69.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 6 p.69"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "ii-chord-in-minor-ii-v-i",
      "narrative": "For the minor ii-V-i, Heussenstamm designates the ii chord as a minor seventh with a flat 5th (half-diminished / min7b5), not a plain min7, and prescribes Locrian as its scale (equivalent to natural minor from the tonic). Chapter 2, p. 10.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
