          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 2 p.10"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v-chord-in-major-ii-v-i",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm fixes the dominant seventh as the V chord in major-key ii-V-I and teaches G Mixolydian as the default scale, noting that Dorian, Mixolydian, and major are all modal siblings sharing the same tones -- making the distinction between them a matter of harmonic context. Chapter 2, p. 9.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 2 p.9"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "natural-vs-altered-decision-rule",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm teaches that when a chord symbol gives only 'F7' or 'Bb7' with no further detail, the most critical interpretive decision is whether to treat it as natural or altered. For V-i motion (dominant resolving to a minor chord), altered is strongly favored; for V-I in major, natural is the default unless the device of switching to altered just before resolution is desired. Chapter 4, p. 30-31.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.30"
        },
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.31"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "natural-to-altered-shift-before-resolution",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes a two-stage scalar move over a resolving dominant: first play from Mixolydian, then switch to super Locrian (altered) immediately before resolution to create urgency -- the distinctive tones of super Locrian (not shared with Mixolydian) mark the pivot. This is demonstrated for both D7 and G7 in Chapter 4, p. 33.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.32"
        },
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.33"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-substitution-chromatic-bass",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes replacing a dom7 with another dom7 rooted a tritone away -- the b5 pivot point where both chords are note-for-note the same (enharmonically respelled) -- producing chromatic root motion in place of circle-of-fifths resolution, and effectively converting a natural dominant into a fully altered one. Chapter 3, p. 17.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.17"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tritone-sub-melodic-descent",
      "narrative": "Over a tritone-substituted dominant, Heussenstamm prescribes descending through scalar tones 5-3-2-1 for each chord, creating an overall down-by-half-steps sound through the progression. Chapter 3, p. 17.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.17"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "whole-ii-v-unit-as-tritone-sub-bebop",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes extending tritone substitution from single chords to entire ii-V pairs -- a bebop-harmony device where a whole substitute ii-V unit replaces the original ii-V, sometimes with the original pair played first and the subs inserted before resolution. Chapter 3, p. 18.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.18"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "parallel-chromatic-turnaround-voicing",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes using tritone-substitute dominant chords for turnarounds, resulting in parallel chromatic movement of dominant-13 voicings (e.g., Db13 replacing G7#9). He emphasizes this is not random sliding but logically derived from circle-of-fifths substitution while retaining original harmonic direction. Chapter 3, p. 18.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.18"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "shell-voicing-middle-strings",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes shell voicings for dominant sevenths -- root, 3rd, 7th reduced to two strings, then root dropped -- on the middle strings for mellow midrange comping alongside a bassist. He notes that tritone-substitute dom7 shells share the exact same two-string shape, with 3rd and 7th inverted. Chapter 5, p. 48-49.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
      "references": [
