    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "bvi-natural-to-v-altered-minor-blues-rule",
      "narrative": "Heussenstamm prescribes a two-chord dominant pattern in minor blues: the bVI chord is a natural dominant (e.g., Bb9 or Bb13), which then leads to the V as an altered dominant (e.g., D7#9 or D7b5). This bVI natural / V altered pairing is presented as 'the normal pattern' for minor blues. Chapter 3, p. 21.",
      "source_principle_ids": [],
      "source_work_id": "goldmine-100-jazz-lessons",
